{% doc %}
  Renders a button, or a link styled as one.

  The distinction matters and is decided by the `href` parameter, not by a style
  choice: something that navigates is an `<a>`, something that acts is a
  `<button>`. Styling a link as a button is fine; making a `<div>` behave like
  one is not, and it is the most common accessibility failure in Shopify themes.

  ## Markup contract

      <a class="button button--primary …">
        <span class="button-text">
          <span class="button__label">Shop now</span>
          <span class="button__icon">…svg…</span>
        </span>
      </a>

  Label and icon travel together inside one `.button-text`. That wrapper is not
  decoration: the swap animation moves label and icon as a single unit, and
  `assets/button-element.js` clones exactly this element to build the track. A
  label and an icon as loose siblings cannot be moved together without a
  wrapper, and adding one at runtime would mean rewriting the button's contents
  after paint.

  ## Hover effects

  `effect` picks how the button reacts, and `auto` picks for it:

    swap   The contents slide across on one continuous travel. A solid badge
           trades places with the label pill; a plain glyph keeps the side it
           started on and travels with the words, the whole group replaced by
           its copy. Either way both label and icon are fully visible at each
           end of the slide.
    sweep  The hover colour wipes across from one side and the contents drift
           with it. Works with any icon or none.
    none

  `auto` takes the swap wherever there is an icon to trade places with, and the
  sweep for a bare label — a label that slides out and back with nothing else
  moving reads as a glitch rather than a response.

  Only `swap` needs JavaScript. `sweep` is a pseudo-element and a transform, so
  it survives a failed script load, and a `swap` button with no script is an
  ordinary button that simply does not move.

  Parameters:
    label     (string, required)  Already translated
    href      (string, optional)  The URL. With `tag: 'auto'` its presence also
                                  picks the element
    tag       (string, optional)  'auto' | 'a' | 'button'. Default 'auto', which
                                  is an `<a>` when there is an `href` and a
                                  `<button>` otherwise. `'a'` without an `href`
                                  falls back to `<button>` — an anchor with no
                                  href is unreachable by keyboard
    variant   (string, optional)  'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline' |
                                  'secondary-outline' | 'link' | 'custom'.
                                  Default primary. 'outline' is the primary
                                  outline; it keeps its old name because every
                                  existing caller passes it
    size      (string, optional)  'small' | 'medium' | 'large' | 'custom'.
                                  Default medium. Sizes are relative to Theme
                                  settings > Buttons > Height, so raising that
                                  scales all four
    custom_height (number, optional) Pixels. Only read when size is 'custom'
    type      (string, optional)  Button type. Default 'button'
    name      (string, optional)  Form control name
    value     (string, optional)  Form control value
    full      (boolean, optional) Full width
    disabled  (boolean, optional)
    loading   (boolean, optional) Shows a spinner and sets aria-busy
    icon      (string, optional)  Icon name from snippets/icon.liquid
    icon_position (string, optional) 'start' | 'end'. Default 'end'
    icon_style (string, optional) 'default' | 'solid'. Solid puts the icon in a
                                  filled circular badge. Default 'default'
    icon_size (number, optional)  Glyph size in px. The solid badge is derived
                                  from it, so both scale together
    icon_radius (number, optional) Badge corner radius as a percentage. 50 is a
                                  circle; 0 is a square
    border_style (string, optional) 'solid' | 'dashed' | 'dotted'
    underline_size (number, optional) Link variant only. Underline thickness in px
    width     (string, optional)  'auto' | 'min' | 'full'. Default 'min'. Auto
                                  hugs the label; min holds a comfortable
                                  minimum; full fills the container
    min_width (number, optional)  Pixels, used when width is 'min'. Default 196
    icon_only (boolean, optional) Hides the label visually. `label` becomes the aria-label
    effect    (string, optional)  'auto' | 'swap' | 'sweep' | 'none'. Default 'auto'
    colors    (string, optional)  Extra custom properties for variant 'custom'
    class     (string, optional)
    id        (string, optional)
    aria_label (string, optional) Overrides the accessible name
    attributes (string, optional) Raw extra attributes, e.g. data-overlay-open="CartDrawer"
    new_tab   (boolean, optional) Links only. Adds rel and a translated hint

  @example
    {% render 'button', label: 'products.product.add_to_cart' | t, type: 'submit', full: true %}

  @example
    {% render 'button',
       label: 'general.actions.view_all' | t,
       href: collection.url,
       variant: 'secondary',
       icon: 'arrow-right',
       icon_style: 'solid' %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign resolved_variant = variant | default: 'primary'
  assign resolved_size = size | default: 'medium'
  comment
    `button--custom` is the custom *colour* variant. A caller asking for the
    custom *height* used to get that same class, so a button set to a custom
    size silently lost its background — two independent choices sharing one
    name, with whichever rule came second winning. The size class is
    `button--custom-size`; callers may pass either spelling.
  endcomment
  if resolved_size == 'custom'
    assign resolved_size = 'custom-size'
  endif
  assign resolved_icon_position = icon_position | default: 'end'
  assign resolved_icon_style = icon_style | default: 'default'
  assign resolved_type = type | default: 'button'
  assign accessible_name = aria_label | default: label

  assign has_icon = false
  if icon != blank and icon != 'none'
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  comment
    An icon style is a fact about an icon, so with no icon there is no style —
    and claiming one is not harmless. `button--icon-solid` is what tells the
    stylesheet to draw a badge, and a button with no icon that still claimed it
    would be styling a circle around nothing.

    The stylesheet cannot defend against this, because from its side the claim
    looks true. So it is settled here, once, where the answer is known.
  endcomment
  unless has_icon
    assign resolved_icon_style = 'default'
  endunless

  comment
    An icon-only button is already the badge: it is a circle the size of the
    button with a glyph in the middle. Claiming `solid` on top asks for a second
    badge inside the first, and the two rule sets then argue over the glyph's
    background, radius and colour — which came out as a square badge floating in
    a round button, and on the filled variants as a glyph painted in the colour
    behind it.

    Settled here for the same reason as above: the stylesheet cannot tell that
    the claim is redundant, but this can.
  endcomment
  if icon_only
    assign resolved_icon_style = 'default'
  endif

  comment
    The swap works with a solid badge, a plain glyph and no icon at all — the
    track is the same in every case, and only what is in the window differs.
    It used to be gated on a solid icon and silently downgraded to the sweep
    otherwise, so choosing "Swap" with a plain arrow appeared to do nothing.

    Auto still prefers the swap wherever there is an icon to trade places with,
    and falls to the sweep for a bare label — a label that slides out and back
    with no colour change reads as a glitch rather than a response.
  endcomment
  assign resolved_effect = effect | default: 'auto'
  if resolved_effect == 'auto'
    if has_icon
      assign resolved_effect = 'swap'
    else
      assign resolved_effect = 'sweep'
    endif
  endif

  comment
    "The icon does not change position" and "the icon does not move" are two
    different requirements, and conflating them is what made this wrong twice.

    A plain glyph keeps its SIDE. It is punctuation on the end of the phrase, so
    pulling it round to the other side breaks the phrase apart — only a solid
    badge is a shape in its own right that can trade places with the label.

    But it still travels. The whole group slides out and its copy slides in,
    icon and words together in the arrangement they started in, so the button
    reads as one thing moving rather than as its parts rearranging.

    The distance is chosen in `assets/base.css` off the classes already on the
    button: a button with an icon stops one icon-box short of a whole group, so
    the icon lands where the label began; a button with nothing to trade with —
    no icon, or icon-only — travels a whole group.
  endcomment

  comment
    The link variant rides the same track as everything else. It used to be
    forced to the sweep and animate its underline instead, which meant the one
    variant merchants reach for most often was the only one that behaved
    differently — and a rule sweeping one way while the words slid the other was
    two animations arguing.

    Its underline now holds still and the text slides under it, so a link reads
    as the same gesture as a button, at the scale of a run of text.
  endcomment

  assign classes = 'button button--' | append: resolved_variant | append: ' button--' | append: resolved_size
  comment
    Neither effect gets a class. The sweep is what a button does by default, so
    it needs no marker; the swap is scoped off the `<button-swap>` wrapper that
    has to exist for it anyway. A class alongside either was a second way of
    saying something the markup already said — and when `button--fx-swap` was
    dropped, every rule still naming it went dead in silence, which is how
    outline icons ended up painted in `transparent`.

    Only "no effect at all" needs stating, and it gets an attribute rather than
    a class: it is a rare exception, not a mode the stylesheet organises around.
  endcomment

  if has_icon
    assign classes = classes | append: ' button--has-icon button--icon-' | append: resolved_icon_position
    if resolved_icon_style == 'solid'
      assign classes = classes | append: ' button--icon-solid'
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Width is a placement decision, not a style one, so it is its own setting
    rather than something inferred from the variant. `full` is kept as a
    separate boolean because thirteen existing callers already pass it.
  endcomment
  assign resolved_width = width | default: 'min'
  if full
    assign classes = classes | append: ' button--full'
  else
    assign classes = classes | append: ' button--width-' | append: resolved_width
  endif
  if icon_only
    assign classes = classes | append: ' button--icon-only'
  endif
  if loading
    assign classes = classes | append: ' button--loading'
  endif
  if class != blank
    assign classes = classes | append: ' ' | append: class
  endif

  comment
    A custom height is one inline custom property, not an inline `height`. The
    stylesheet still owns the rule; this only supplies the number, so hover,
    focus and the responsive rules keep working. Nothing is emitted for the
    other sizes, which keeps the markup clean on the common path.
  endcomment
  assign inline_style = ''
  comment
    The block sends `custom-size` because `button--custom` already means the
    custom *colour* variant, and one class name for two independent choices
    meant whichever came second silently won. Both spellings are accepted so a
    direct caller passing `custom` still gets its height.
  endcomment
  if resolved_size == 'custom-size'
    if custom_height != blank
      assign inline_style = inline_style | append: '--button-custom-height:' | append: custom_height | append: 'px;'
    endif
  endif
  if icon_size != blank
    assign inline_style = inline_style | append: '--button-icon-size:' | append: icon_size | append: 'px;'
  endif
  comment
    The badge is derived from the glyph size, so a merchant turning the icon
    down gets a smaller circle rather than a small arrow rattling around inside
    an unchanged one. Only the radius needs stating.
  endcomment
  if icon_radius != blank
    assign inline_style = inline_style | append: '--button-icon-radius:' | append: icon_radius | append: '%;'
  endif
  if border_style != blank and border_style != 'default'
    assign inline_style = inline_style | append: '--button-border-style:' | append: border_style | append: ';'
  endif
  comment
    The link variant has no border, so Border thickness would otherwise be a
    dead control on it. It drives the underline instead, which is the only rule
    that variant draws.
  endcomment
  if min_width != blank and resolved_width == 'min'
    assign inline_style = inline_style | append: '--button-min-width:' | append: min_width | append: 'px;'
  endif
  if resolved_variant == 'link' and underline_size != blank
    assign inline_style = inline_style | append: '--button-underline-size:' | append: underline_size | append: 'px;'
  endif
  if colors != blank
    assign inline_style = inline_style | append: colors
  endif
-%}

{%- capture button_content -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    One wrapper around both, always — including when there is no icon. The swap
    clones this element, and a structure that changes shape depending on whether
    an icon happens to be present is a structure the stylesheet has to guess at.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <span class="button-text">
    {%- if has_icon and resolved_icon_position == 'start' -%}
      <span class="button__icon button__icon--start">
        {% render 'icon', icon: icon %}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}

    <span class="button__label{% if icon_only %} visually-hidden{% endif %}">{{ label }}</span>

    {%- if has_icon and resolved_icon_position == 'end' -%}
      <span class="button__icon button__icon--end">
        {% render 'icon', icon: icon %}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </span>

  {%- if loading -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The spinner is decorative and sits outside `.button-text`, so a cloned
      track never carries two of them. The state is announced by aria-busy on
      the control itself, so a screen reader is told the button is working
      rather than being read a spinning graphic.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span class="button__spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Which element gets rendered is its own decision, made once, here.

    It used to be inferred from whether `href` was present, which quietly tied
    two unrelated things together: a caller who had no URL yet got a `<button>`
    they did not ask for, and a caller who wanted a real `<button>` had to make
    sure they passed no URL. `tag` states it instead, and `auto` keeps the old
    convenience for the callers that are happy with it.

    The one combination that is never rendered is an `<a>` with no `href`. That
    is not a link and not a button: it is invisible to the keyboard, has no
    role, and does nothing when activated. Asking for it gets a `<button>`,
    because a control the keyboard cannot reach is the failure this snippet
    exists to prevent.
  endcomment
  assign resolved_tag = tag | default: 'auto'
  if resolved_tag == 'auto'
    if href != blank
      assign resolved_tag = 'a'
    else
      assign resolved_tag = 'button'
    endif
  endif
  if resolved_tag == 'a' and href == blank
    assign resolved_tag = 'button'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture button_markup -%}
  {%- if resolved_tag == 'a' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      A disabled link keeps its place in the tab order. `tabindex="-1"` was
      taking it out, which is the standard way to make a control impossible to
      find with a keyboard: it vanishes from the sequence with nothing
      announcing why, and a customer tabbing through the page simply never
      reaches it. `aria-disabled` announces the state and leaves it reachable;
      the stylesheet blocks the pointer, and pointer-events does not affect
      keyboard focus.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a
      {% if id != blank %}id="{{ id }}"{% endif %}
      class="{{ classes }}"
      {% if inline_style != blank %}style="{{ inline_style }}"{% endif %}
      href="{{ href }}"
      {% if icon_only %}aria-label="{{ accessible_name | escape }}"{% endif %}
      {% if new_tab %}target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% endif %}
      {% if disabled %}aria-disabled="true"{% endif %}
      {% if resolved_effect == 'none' %}data-fx="none"{% endif %}
      {{ attributes }}
    >
      {{ button_content }}
      {%- if new_tab -%}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'accessibility.new_window' | t }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </a>
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `disabled` rather than `aria-disabled` here, because a native disabled
      button is already handled correctly by every browser and assistive
      technology — it is skipped, announced, and cannot be submitted.

      An `href` on a `<button>` is not a mistake worth silently dropping: it
      usually means the caller wanted a link. It becomes a `data-href` so an
      app or a listener can still find it, rather than being lost.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <button
      {% if id != blank %}id="{{ id }}"{% endif %}
      class="{{ classes }}"
      {% if inline_style != blank %}style="{{ inline_style }}"{% endif %}
      type="{{ resolved_type }}"
      {% if name != blank %}name="{{ name }}"{% endif %}
      {% if value != blank %}value="{{ value | escape }}"{% endif %}
      {% if href != blank %}data-href="{{ href }}"{% endif %}
      {% if icon_only %}aria-label="{{ accessible_name | escape }}"{% endif %}
      {% if disabled %}disabled{% endif %}
      {% if loading %}aria-busy="true"{% endif %}
      {% if resolved_effect == 'none' %}data-fx="none"{% endif %}
      {{ attributes }}
    >
      {{ button_content }}
    </button>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The custom element wraps rather than replaces. Everything inside is a working
  button before the script runs and stays one if it never does — the element
  only measures the track and injects the clone. Buttons that do not swap are
  not wrapped at all, so no page pays for a component it does not use.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if resolved_effect == 'swap' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The wrapper carries no icon information any more. It used to repeat position,
    style and icon-only as data attributes, and the stylesheet read those instead
    of the classes already on the button — three restatements of three facts, and
    three chances to disagree. They did: a button with no icon still announced
    `data-icon-style="solid"` and took a travel meant for a badge it did not
    have, which is what put the label off-centre.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <button-swap class="button-swap"{% if full %} data-full{% endif %}>{{ button_markup }}</button-swap>
{%- else -%}
  {{ button_markup }}
{%- endif -%}
